#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783c067b-9676-57f1-81a9-7724c18e1534" :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Variables in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "5d89b84d-71cc-5c63-96f1-8abc8e2b2ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14140" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac 9 8$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac 9 8 \\\\[5pt] 3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot 9 \\\\[5pt] 24 &= 18x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 24 &= 18x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{24}{18} &= \\dfrac{18x}{18} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{4}{3} &= x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20c12caf-9f3e-5dcb-a12c-5c368824ede9" :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "4a26be93-e244-596b-9341-383dbbbc3e88" :question/math-academy-id "e-14142" :question/problem "Solve $-\\dfrac{2}{5y} = \\dfrac{1}{10}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\ \\dfrac{-2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10} \\\\ (-2) \\cdot 10 &= 5y \\cdot 1 \\\\ -20 &= 5y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -20 &= 5y \\ \\dfrac{-20}{5} &= y \\\\ -4 &= y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y = -4$ is different from $y=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $y=-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8f96a06-1ebb-5ed1-9e47-23525e2a756e" :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Binomials in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd8760f-af04-5196-90d6-0d4cfdfaec2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14145" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} = \\dfrac 9 8$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator $2x+1$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have\n\n$$\n2x+1=0\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} &= \\dfrac 9 8 \\\\[5pt] 3x \\cdot 8 &= (2x+1) \\cdot 9 \\\\[5pt] 24x &= 18x + 9 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 24x &= 18x + 9 \\\\[5pt] 24x-18x &= 18x + 9 -18x \\\\[5pt] 6x& = 9 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{6x}{6} & = \\dfrac{9}{6} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{3}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis different from\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20c12caf-9f3e-5dcb-a12c-5c368824ede9" :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "ddf5e515-6b35-58f4-a8f8-583ce3852bf3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080767}] :question/id #uuid "3dabaa4a-5173-540d-876b-8e293aec78da" :question/math-academy-id "q-217272" :question/problem "The solution to $-\\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{2}{6t}$ is$t =$\n{{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref